<BaseLayout title="BL60 (Kunlun) — Kunlun Mountains Acupressure Point" description="Learn how to locate and stimulate BL60 (Kunlun) — the Kunlun Mountains point. Benefits include leg pain relief, headache relief, and labor support.">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">BL60 (Kunlun) — Kunlun Mountains</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">BL60, known as Kunlun or Kunlun Mountains, is located behind the outer ankle. It is used for leg and foot pain, headaches, and traditionally for regulating labor.</p>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/bl60-location.png" alt="BL60 acupressure point location behind the outer ankle" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" />
    <p class="mb-3">BL60 is in the depression between the lateral malleolus (outer ankle bone) and the Achilles tendon, level with the top of the ankle bone. It lies on the Bladder meridian.</p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Leg and foot pain:</strong> BL60 relieves pain along the Bladder meridian, including sciatica and plantar fasciitis.</li>
      <li><strong>Headaches:</strong> It is effective for occipital headaches and tension headaches radiating from the neck.</li>
      <li><strong>Achilles tendon discomfort:</strong> BL60 helps with Achilles tendonitis and ankle sprains.</li>
      <li><strong>Labor support:</strong> In traditional Chinese medicine, BL60 is used to promote labor and ease delivery.</li>
      <li><strong>Heel pain:</strong> It is a key point for managing heel spurs and plantar fasciitis pain.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">How to Massage BL60</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Sit with your leg crossed so your ankle is accessible.</li>
      <li>Place your thumb on the BL60 point behind your outer ankle.</li>
      <li>Apply firm pressure with a circular or steady pushing motion.</li>
      <li>Hold for 30–60 seconds, then repeat on the other leg.</li>
      <li>Use gentle ankle rotations after massage for best results.</li>
    </ol>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2018 study in <em>Acupuncture &amp; Electro-Therapeutics Research</em> found that BL60 stimulation increased pain threshold and reduced pain intensity in patients with chronic ankle pain.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30001243/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1</a></sup> Research in <em>Journal of Traditional Chinese Medicine</em> demonstrated that BL60 acupuncture was effective for treating plantar heel pain.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25842707/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2</a></sup></p>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2021 review in <em>Complementary Therapies in Clinical Practice</em> found that BL60 combined with other points reduced labor duration and pain during childbirth.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34089926/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3</a></sup></p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Use with caution during pregnancy:</strong> BL60 may stimulate uterine contractions and should only be used during labor.</li>
      <li>Avoid if you have an ankle fracture, severe sprain, or gout in the ankle.</li>
      <li>Do not massage over varicose veins or deep vein thrombosis.</li>
      <li>Use gentle pressure — the area between the ankle bone and Achilles is sensitive.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Related Points</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">BL60 pairs well with <a href="/points/bl40-weizhong-acupressure-point/">BL40 (Weizhong)</a> for sciatica and back pain. For headaches, combine with <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/">GB20 (Fengchi)</a> and <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/">LI4 (Hegu)</a>.</p>
  </section></article>
</BaseLayout>
